WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.178
Industrial protocols are the communication rules that connect controllers, HMIs, sensors, actuators, historians, engineering tools, and supervisory systems.

00:00:10.178 --> 00:00:18.790
Their messages carry operational data and, in many cases, actions that can affect physical processes.

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:30.011
Common patterns include polling, command and response, publish and subscribe, controller coordination, historian collection, supervisory control, and privileged engineering access.

00:00:30.011 --> 00:00:37.579
Defenders can use these stable relationships to establish baselines and identify meaningful change.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:45.669
Modbus offers simple reads and writes but often relies on external controls for trust.

00:00:45.669 --> 00:00:51.671
DNP3 supports distributed telemetry and control, including efficient event reporting.

00:00:51.671 --> 00:01:03.415
OPC technologies enable interoperable data exchange, while OPC UA adds modern security capabilities that still require sound certificate and configuration management.

00:01:03.415 --> 00:01:12.549
EtherNet/IP and PROFINET operate within engineered vendor ecosystems where timing, supported configurations, and device roles matter.

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:21.161
Across every protocol, technical interpretation must include process state, architecture, user authority, and planned maintenance.

00:01:21.161 --> 00:01:32.382
Good protection combines passive protocol-aware monitoring, segmentation, controlled engineering and remote access, secure configuration, change management, and coordinated incident response.

00:01:32.382 --> 00:01:38.906
Many industrial protocols were designed for reliability before current cybersecurity expectations.

00:01:38.906 --> 00:01:50.128
Effective security respects that history without accepting unmanaged risk, bringing security, operations, and engineering together around evidence and safe action.
